  "id": "Compound::DB00786 - Gene::4322",
  "verbalization": "Marimastat, a small molecule inhibitor, is shown to bind to multiple MMPs (Matrix Metalloproteinases) such as Mmp23b, Mmp26, and Mmp27, which are all members of the MMP family. These MMPs are known to play a critical role in the degradation of extracellular matrix components, and their activity is often dysregulated in various pathological conditions. The evidence suggests that Marimastat exerts its effects through a common mechanism of binding to these MMPs, which are then involved in the process of matrix degradation. The shared participation of these MMPs in the same pathway, as indicated by the \"is participated by\" relationship with Mmp13, implies that Marimastat may inhibit Mmp13 through a similar mechanism, potentially by suppressing its activity or preventing its interaction with the extracellular matrix. This integrated evidence supports a plausible mechanism by which Marimastat could modulate Mmp13, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction and functional impact on Mmp13.",
